WEBVTT

00:00:08.803 --> 00:00:14.603
Our keynote speaker is a man who I'm sure
is very well known to all of you. He's Professor

00:00:14.603 --> 00:00:20.373
of Fruitology at Dubbo University and has
written many books on the subject of tropical

00:00:20.373 --> 00:00:27.533
fruit. So without further ado, I'd like to
introduce our keynote speaker, Doctor Sam

00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:28.533
Eriks.

00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:37.112
Thankyou Denise.
The Honourable Judith Bryant, Minister for

00:00:37.112 --> 00:00:42.473
Trade, Professor Eric Vogel, Professor of
Economics at Wagga University, distinguished

00:00:42.473 --> 00:00:49.682
guests, ladies and gentlemen. Today's topic
'why bananas are bent' is a very significant

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:55.383
one in terms both of international trade,
and culture. In thinking about the topic,

00:00:55.383 --> 00:01:00.523
I felt it would be appropriate to address
briefly the history of bananas and banana

00:01:00.523 --> 00:01:06.643
farming, the many qualities of bananas, both
positive and negative, and of course examine

00:01:06.643 --> 00:01:12.113
the uses of the banana.
But first let me tell you a story about a

00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:14.813
banana.

00:01:14.813 --> 00:01:19.993
Ladies and gentlemen, I hope I've been able
to clear up a few misconceptions, and leave

00:01:19.993 --> 00:01:25.583
you with some new ideas about how we might
view bananas in the future.

00:01:25.583 --> 00:01:31.922
We've seen, in looking at their history, that
bananas have a significant role in many cultures.

00:01:31.922 --> 00:01:38.333
I've also noted their positive nutritional
qualities. And in addressing the main question,

00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:44.613
why bananas are bent, we've learned that the
reasons are many and complex.

00:01:44.613 --> 00:01:50.083
Madam Chair, thank you for the opportunity
to address the conference today, and thank

00:01:50.083 --> 00:02:02.503
you ladies and gentleman for your kind attention.
Making a formal speech to an audience is a

00:02:02.503 --> 00:02:08.072
scary thing for many people - even more so
if it's in a language that is not your first

00:02:08.072 --> 00:02:15.233
language. What are the things you can do to
prepare a formal speech in English? First

00:02:15.233 --> 00:02:21.713
of all, let's look at the structure of the
speech. In a formal situation, like a keynote

00:02:21.713 --> 00:02:26.453
address, the speaker will be introduced by
someone else.

00:02:26.453 --> 00:02:32.613
Our keynote speaker is a man who I'm sure
is very well known to all of you. He's Professor

00:02:32.613 --> 00:02:38.383
of Fruitology at Dubbo University and has
written many books on the subject of tropical

00:02:38.383 --> 00:02:41.973
fruit.
When introducing a speaker, research their

00:02:41.973 --> 00:02:47.553
background and accomplishments - that is,
the important things they've done, such as

00:02:47.553 --> 00:02:52.983
books they may have written, important positions
they've filled, and of course their proper

00:02:52.983 --> 00:02:57.603
title or qualifications, such as Professor.

00:02:57.603 --> 00:03:04.622
Here are some useful phrases to use when introducing
a speaker. Practise them with Denise:

00:03:04.622 --> 00:03:09.913
Our next speaker is well known to all of you.

00:03:09.913 --> 00:03:12.622
Our next speaker needs no introduction.

00:03:12.622 --> 00:03:21.253
Without further ado, I'd like to introduce

00:03:21.253 --> 00:03:30.983
Please make him welcome, Doctor Sam Eriks.
When giving a formal speech to an audience,

00:03:30.983 --> 00:03:37.822
we need to be aware of protocol. Protocol
means the proper or customary way of doing

00:03:37.822 --> 00:03:44.953
things in formal situations. Part of the protocol
for a formal speech is addressing the audience

00:03:44.953 --> 00:03:50.783
at the beginning. A keynote speaker needs
to know who the important people are at the

00:03:50.783 --> 00:03:57.133
meeting, and address them using their formal
titles, starting with the most important people.

00:03:57.133 --> 00:03:58.233
Thank you Denise.

00:03:58.233 --> 00:04:03.343
The Honourable Judith Bryant, Minister for
Trade, Professor Eric Vogel, Professor of

00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:15.043
economics at Wagga University, distinguished
guests, ladies and gentlemen.

00:04:15.043 --> 00:04:19.663
If there is a representative of government,
such as a minister, they would be acknowledged

00:04:19.663 --> 00:04:23.842
first, then any other people of particular
note.

00:04:23.842 --> 00:04:28.183
Include their title, name and position.

00:04:28.183 --> 00:04:35.442
Then he addresses 'distinguished guests' - this
can include anyone who has been invited to

00:04:35.442 --> 00:04:43.273
attend the event. And finally he says 'ladies
and gentlemen', which means everyone else.

00:04:43.273 --> 00:04:47.703
What does Doctor Eriks do next?
In thinking about the topic, I felt it would

00:04:47.703 --> 00:04:54.273
be appropriate to address briefly the history
of bananas and banana farming, the many qualities

00:04:54.273 --> 00:05:00.883
of bananas, both positive and negative, and
of course examine the uses of the banana.

00:05:00.883 --> 00:05:08.233
He outlines the three main parts of his speech.
Listen to him again. What are the three parts

00:05:08.233 --> 00:05:12.013
of his talk?
I felt it would be appropriate to address

00:05:12.013 --> 00:05:17.942
briefly the history of bananas and banana
farming, the many qualities of bananas, both

00:05:17.942 --> 00:05:22.953
positive and negative, and of course examine
the uses of the banana.

00:05:22.953 --> 00:05:29.893
The first one is the history of bananas and
banana farming, the second one is the many

00:05:29.893 --> 00:05:37.803
qualities of bananas, and the third one is
the uses of the banana. In listing things

00:05:37.803 --> 00:05:43.173
like this in a speech, it's important to use
pauses in speech so that the audience can

00:05:43.173 --> 00:05:46.423
follow and hear the three points.

00:05:46.423 --> 00:05:51.153
How does it sound without pauses?
I felt it would be appropriate to address

00:05:51.153 --> 00:05:55.513
briefly the history of bananas and banana
farming, the many qualities of bananas, both

00:05:55.513 --> 00:05:59.502
positive and negative, and of course examine
the uses of the banana.

00:05:59.502 --> 00:06:06.192
In making a speech, it's important to use
pauses to help make your point. In the list,

00:06:06.192 --> 00:06:12.693
pause before each point in the list. Pause
between sentences, and before making a major

00:06:12.693 --> 00:06:19.763
point, like this:
The point I want to make is this: not all

00:06:19.763 --> 00:06:25.553
bananas are bent.
Stress and intonation are important too. In

00:06:25.553 --> 00:06:31.383
saying, not all bananas are bent, Doctor Eriks
stresses the word 'all' because it is the

00:06:31.383 --> 00:06:37.692
most important word in that statement. In
listing the three parts of his speech, notice

00:06:37.692 --> 00:06:43.353
how his intonation is rising in the first
two parts, and then falling for the last.

00:06:43.353 --> 00:06:47.692
This indicates to the audience he has finished
the list:

00:06:47.692 --> 00:06:55.963
The history of bananas, the many qualities
of bananas, and the uses of the banana.

00:06:55.963 --> 00:07:01.893
In describing his topic, he said, "I felt
it would be appropriate to address" and then

00:07:01.893 --> 00:07:08.383
names the parts of his speech. To 'address'
something here means to talk about it. You

00:07:08.383 --> 00:07:15.513
could also use words like consider, discuss,
outline, cover.

00:07:15.513 --> 00:07:22.043
Pronunciation is important too - it's a good
idea to practise your speech out loud, especially

00:07:22.043 --> 00:07:28.103
any difficult words.
I've also noted their positive nutritionist,

00:07:28.103 --> 00:07:35.472
nutrition, nutritional qualities.
What does Doctor Eriks do next in his speech?

00:07:35.472 --> 00:07:40.103
But first let me tell you a story about a
banana.

00:07:40.103 --> 00:07:45.213
He says he is going to tell a story about
a banana. When making a speech, it's good

00:07:45.213 --> 00:07:51.633
to put in some personal touches - a story
of something that happened or a joke.

00:07:51.633 --> 00:07:56.493
We move now to the end of Sam's speech. How
does he finish?

00:07:56.493 --> 00:08:01.432
Ladies and gentlemen, I hope I've been able
to clear up a few misconceptions about bananas,

00:08:01.432 --> 00:08:07.273
and leave you with some new ideas about how
we might view bananas in the future.

00:08:07.273 --> 00:08:13.472
First, he signals that he is ending his speech,
by repeating 'ladies and gentlemen'. Then

00:08:13.472 --> 00:08:19.872
he says, "I hope I've been able to clear up
a few misconceptions". By using the present

00:08:19.872 --> 00:08:26.913
perfect, 'I have been able' he signals that
he is talking about his speech up to now.

00:08:26.913 --> 00:08:31.363
Practise with Doctor Eriks some ways of signalling
the end of a speech:

00:08:31.363 --> 00:08:37.463
I hope I've been able to clarify the issue.

00:08:37.463 --> 00:08:44.023
I hope I've addressed the major concerns about
this issue.

00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:49.543
Next he restates the major points he's made.
We've seen, in looking at their history, that

00:08:49.543 --> 00:08:56.533
bananas have a significant role in many cultures.
I've also noted their positive nutritional

00:08:56.533 --> 00:09:02.633
qualities. And in addressing the main question,
why bananas are bent, we've learned that the

00:09:02.633 --> 00:09:08.403
reasons are many and complex.
Notice the use of the present perfect in re-stating

00:09:08.403 --> 00:09:09.923
these points.

00:09:09.923 --> 00:09:13.193
we've seen
I've noted

00:09:13.193 --> 00:09:15.843
we've learned

00:09:15.843 --> 00:09:19.082
There are other phrases that could be used
in this way:

00:09:19.082 --> 00:09:22.693
We've observed
I've outlined

00:09:22.693 --> 00:09:26.813
I've referred to, and so on.

00:09:26.813 --> 00:09:30.383
Finally, how does Doctor Eriks wrap up his
speech?

00:09:30.383 --> 00:09:35.843
Madam Chair, thank you for the opportunity
to address the conference today, and thank

00:09:35.843 --> 00:09:41.323
you ladies and gentleman for your kind attention.
Well, there's a lot more we can say about

00:09:41.323 --> 00:09:46.303
making formal speeches, but I hope you've
learned some useful tips today. Thank you

00:09:46.303 --> 00:09:50.323
for your attention, and I'll see you next
time for The Business of English.